.TH QUILT 1 2026-10-19 quilt.cpp
.SH NAME
quilt \- manage a series of patches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY quilt
.RB [ \-\-quiltrc
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-trace [ =verbose ]]
.I command
.RI [ option " ...]"
.RI [ argument " ...]"
.YS
.SY quilt
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.YS
.SY quilt
.I command
.B \-\-help
.YS
.SY quilt
.B \-\-version
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quilt
manages a stack of patches against a source tree.
Each patch is a file in the patches directory, and the
.I series
file lists the patches in the order they apply.
.B quilt push
applies the next patch and
.B quilt pop
removes the last one applied.
The last patch applied is the
.IR "topmost patch" ,
which most commands work on unless told otherwise.
.PP
For each file in an applied patch, quilt keeps a copy of the file as it
was before the patch, in the
.I .pc
directory.
The copy is made when the patch is applied, or when the file is added to the
patch with
.B quilt add
or
.BR "quilt edit" ,
so a file must be added to the topmost patch before it is changed.
.B quilt refresh
compares each copy with the current file and writes the differences, after
the patch's header, to the patch file.
Until then the changes are recorded nowhere.
.PP
quilt.cpp has its own diff and patch engines.
It runs no other programs, except an editor and a utility given to
.BR "quilt diff \%\-\-diff" .
.PP
Each command first finds the root of the tree: the current directory, or the
nearest one above it, that holds the
.I .pc
directory or the patches directory.
If there is none, the current directory is the root.
Commands work from the root, but file names given to them are relative to
the current directory, so quilt can be used anywhere in the tree.
.PP
A patch is named by its path in the patches directory, such as
.B fix.patch
or
.BR crash/null.diff ,
and quilt adds no suffix of its own.
A name may also begin with the patches directory, as quilt prints names when
.B \%QUILT_PATCHES_PREFIX
is set.
A command may be abbreviated to any prefix that names no other command, such
as
.B ref
for
.BR refresh .
.SH OPTIONS
These options may appear anywhere on the command line.
The first argument that does not begin with
.B \-
names the command, and the other arguments go to the command in order, so
.B quilt \-a push
is
.BR "quilt push \-a" .
.TP
\fB\-\-quiltrc\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-quiltrc=\fIfile\fR
Read
.I file
instead of the usual configuration file.
With
.BR \- ,
or with no value at the end of the command line, read none.
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\fR[\fB=verbose\fR]
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \%\-\-help
Without a command, list the commands, as
.B quilt help
does.
.TP
.B \%\-\-version
Print the version, such as
.BR 0.69 ,
when it is the only argument.
.PP
Commands parse options in the manner of
.BR \%getopt_long (3).
Short options may be grouped, so
.B \-fa
is
.BR "\-f \-a" .
A required value is attached or is the next argument, whatever that is:
.B \-p0
or
.BR "\-p 0" .
An optional value, shown as
.RI [ value ]
right after its option, must be attached:
.B \-z
or
.BR \-zfix.patch .
A long option takes its value after
.BR = ,
or as the next argument when the value is required, and may be shortened
to any unique prefix, such as
.B \%\-\-leave
for
.BR \%\-\-leave\-rejects .
Options and operands may come in any order, and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Every command accepts
.B \-h
and
.BR \%\-\-help ,
which print its usage.
.PP
Each command reads default arguments from the variable
.BI \%QUILT_ COMMAND _ARGS\fR,
such as
.BR \%QUILT_PUSH_ARGS ,
which come before those on the command line; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.SH COMMANDS
The commands, by task:
.TP 10
Stack
.BR series ,
.BR applied ,
.BR unapplied ,
.BR top ,
.BR next ,
.BR previous ,
.BR push ,
.B pop
.TP
Changes
.BR new ,
.BR add ,
.BR edit ,
.BR remove ,
.BR revert ,
.BR refresh ,
.BR diff ,
.BR fold ,
.B snapshot
.TP
Patches
.BR delete ,
.BR rename ,
.BR fork ,
.BR import ,
.BR header ,
.B mail
.TP
Queries
.BR files ,
.BR patches ,
.BR annotate ,
.B graph
.TP
Other
.BR init ,
.BR upgrade ,
and the unimplemented
.BR grep ,
.BR setup ,
and
.B shell
.PP
They are described below in alphabetical order.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS add
.SY "quilt add"
[\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR]
\fIfile\fR ...
.YS
.PP
Add each
.I file
to the topmost patch, or to the named applied
.IR patch ,
by saving a copy of it.
A file that does not exist yet becomes a new file in the patch.
quilt reports a file that is already in the patch, that a patch applied
later also changes, that lies in the patches or
.I .pc
directory, or that is a directory or a symbolic link, and goes on with the
rest.
A read-only file is made writable.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS annotate
.SY "quilt annotate"
[\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR]
\fIfile\fR
.YS
.PP
Print
.I file
with a number and a tab before each line.
The number stands for the last applied patch that changed the line, and is
left out for lines that no patch changed.
A legend that gives the name for each number follows, after a blank line.
With
.BR \-P ,
show the file as it was after the named applied
.IR patch .
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS applied
.SY "quilt applied"
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
List the applied patches from the bottom of the stack up, or only those up
to the named
.IR patch .
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS delete
.SY "quilt delete"
[\fB\-r\fR]
[\fB\-\-backup\fR]
[\fIpatch\fR|\fB\-n\fR]
.YS
.PP
Remove the topmost patch, or the named
.IR patch ,
from the series.
An applied patch can be deleted only when it is the topmost: it is popped
first, and changes not yet refreshed into it are lost.
.TP
.B \-r
Remove the patch file too.
.TP
.B \%\-\-backup
With
.BR \-r ,
rename the patch file to
.IB patch \(ti
instead.
.TP
.B \-n
Delete the patch after the topmost patch, or the first patch when none is
applied.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS diff
.SY "quilt diff"
[\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR|\fB\-p ab\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR|\fB\-U\fR \fInum\fR|\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-C\fR \fInum\fR]
[\fB\-\-combine\fR \fIpatch\fR|\fB\-z\fR]
[\fB\-R\fR]
[\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR]
[\fB\-\-snapshot\fR]
[\fB\-\-diff=\fIutility\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-timestamps\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-index\fR]
[\fB\-\-sort\fR]
[\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]]
[\fB\-\-diff\-algorithm=\fIname\fR]
[\fIfile\fR ...]
.YS
.PP
Print the changes in the topmost patch as a diff, without writing
anything: each file's saved copy against the file as it is now.
With
.IR file s,
print only those files.
When a patch applied later also changes a file, the diff for that file
stops at that patch's copy, and a warning follows.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR, \fB\-p ab\fR
Label files in the style of
.B \-p0
or
.BR \-p1 ,
or with
.B a/
and
.B b/
prefixes; see
.BR "Patch files" .
The default is the patch's strip level.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-U\fR \fInum\fR, \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-C\fR \fInum\fR
Write a unified diff, the default, or a context diff, with 3 or
.I num
lines of context.
.TP
\fB\-\-combine\fR \fIpatch\fR
Print the patches from
.I patch
through the topmost or named patch as one diff, where
.B \-
is the first applied patch.
.TP
.B \-z
Print only the changes made since the patch was last refreshed.
.TP
.B \-R
Reverse the diff.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR
Diff the named applied
.I patch
instead of the topmost.
.TP
.B \%\-\-snapshot
Diff the files in the snapshot and in the applied patches against the
snapshot taken by
.BR "quilt snapshot" .
.TP
\fB\-\-diff=\fIutility\fR
For each changed file, run
.IR utility ,
split into words at blanks, with the old and new file names, and print its
output instead.
A missing file is passed as
.BR /dev/null .
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-timestamps\fR, \fB\-\-no\-index\fR
Leave out the timestamps, or the
.B Index:
lines.
.TP
.B \%\-\-sort
Sort the files by name, instead of following the patch file.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]
Accepted and ignored, since quilt.cpp never colors its output.
.I when
must be
.BR always ,
.BR auto ,
.BR tty ,
.BR never ,
or empty.
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\-algorithm=\fIname\fR
Use the diff algorithm
.IR name ,
instead of the one from
.BR \%QUILT_DIFF_ALGORITHM .
This option is a quilt.cpp extension.
.PP
.BR \%\-\-combine ,
.BR \%\-\-snapshot ,
and
.B \-z
exclude one another.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS edit
.SY "quilt edit"
\fIfile\fR ...
.YS
.PP
Add each
.I file
to the topmost patch, unless it is already there, then open them all in the
editor named by
.BR EDITOR ,
or
.BR vi .
The exit status is the editor's.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS files
.SY "quilt files"
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-a\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR]
[\fB\-\-combine\fR \fIpatch\fR]
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
List the files that the topmost or named patch changes, sorted by name.
For an unapplied patch, the names come from its patch file.
.TP
.B \-a
List the files of each patch from the first applied patch through the
topmost or named one.
.TP
.B \-l
Put the patch name before each file name.
.TP
.B \-v
Mark files the patch creates with
.B +
and files it deletes with
.BR \- ,
and with
.BR \-a ,
print each patch's name above its files.
With
.BR \-l ,
it puts the patch name in brackets instead.
.TP
\fB\-\-combine\fR \fIpatch\fR
Like
.BR \-a ,
but start at
.IR patch ,
where
.B \-
is the first applied patch.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS fold
.SY "quilt fold"
[\fB\-R\fR]
[\fB\-q\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR]
.YS
.PP
Apply a patch read from standard input in the current directory, as part
of the topmost patch: the files it changes are added to the topmost patch
first, and its changes reach the patch file on the next refresh.
If it does not apply cleanly, every file is put back as it was.
.TP
.B \-R
Apply the patch in reverse.
.TP
.B \-q
Print only failures.
.TP
.B \-f
Keep the hunks that apply, and write the others to
.IB file .rej
files.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR
Strip
.I n
leading directories from file names, 1 by default.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS fork
.SY "quilt fork"
[\fInew_name\fR]
.YS
.PP
Replace the topmost patch in the series with a copy of it named
.IR new_name ,
so that it can be changed while the original patch file stays as it is,
such as for another series that uses it.
Without
.IR new_name ,
a
.BI \- N
in front of the suffix counts up, or else
.B \-2
is added there:
.B fix.patch
becomes
.BR fix\-2.patch ,
then
.BR fix\-3.patch .
The suffixes are
.BR .diff ,
.BR .dif ,
and
.BR .patch ,
with or without a compression suffix such as
.BR .gz .
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS graph
.SY "quilt graph"
[\fB\-\-all\fR]
[\fB\-\-reduce\fR]
[\fB\-\-lines\fR[\fB=\fInum\fR]]
[\fB\-\-edge\-labels=files\fR]
[\fB\-T ps\fR]
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Print a graph of how the applied patches depend on each other, in the
language of
.BR dot (1).
A patch depends on the closest earlier patch that changes the same file.
The graph holds the topmost or named patch, drawn in bold, and the patches
connected to it through the files it changes: those it depends on and
those that depend on it.
.TP
.B \%\-\-all
Graph all applied patches, leaving out those with no dependency.
.TP
.B \%\-\-reduce
Leave out each edge that a longer path already implies.
.TP
\fB\-\-lines\fR[\fB=\fInum\fR]
Count a dependency only where the changes overlap, given
.I num
lines of context, 2 by default.
.TP
.B \%\-\-edge\-labels=files
Label each edge with the names of the files involved.
.TP
.B \-T ps
Not implemented.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS header
.SY "quilt header"
[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-e\fR]
[\fB\-\-backup\fR]
[\fB\-\-dep3\fR]
[\fB\-\-strip\-diffstat\fR]
[\fB\-\-strip\-trailing\-whitespace\fR]
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Print the header of the topmost or named patch, the text before the first
diff in its patch file, which usually describes the patch.
.TP
.B \-a
Append standard input to the header.
.TP
.B \-r
Replace the header with standard input.
.TP
.B \-e
Edit the header with
.BR EDITOR ,
or
.BR vi .
.TP
.B \%\-\-backup
When changing the header, keep the old patch file as
.IB patch \(ti .
.TP
.B \%\-\-dep3
When editing an empty header, start from a template for the DEP-3 fields
that Debian uses to describe patches.
.TP
.B \%\-\-strip\-diffstat
Remove any diffstat from the header.
.TP
.B \%\-\-strip\-trailing\-whitespace
Remove trailing whitespace from the lines of the header.
.PP
Without
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-r ,
or
.BR \-e ,
the last two options change only what is printed.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS import
.SY "quilt import"
[\fB\-p\fR \fInum\fR]
[\fB\-R\fR]
[\fB\-P\fR \fIname\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR]
[\fB\-d\fR \fBo\fR|\fBa\fR|\fBn\fR]
\fIpatchfile\fR ...
.YS
.PP
Copy each
.I patchfile
into the patches directory, and add the patches in order to the series,
after the topmost patch or at the start when none is applied.
They are not applied.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fInum\fR
Record strip level
.I num
in the series.
.TP
.B \-R
Record that the patch applies in reverse.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR \fIname\fR
Store the patch as
.IR name ,
instead of under the base name of its file.
Only one
.I patchfile
may be given.
.TP
.B \-f
Replace a patch of the same name that is in the series but not applied,
keeping its series entry.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR \fBo\fR|\fBa\fR|\fBn\fR
When replacing a patch, keep the old header, both headers, or the new one.
Without
.BR \-d ,
the old header is kept if the new patch has none, and headers that differ
are an error that shows the difference.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS init
.SY "quilt init"
.YS
.PP
Make the current directory the root of a tree: create the
.I .pc
directory, the patches directory, and an empty series file, leaving any that
exist alone.
This is useful before working from subdirectories, since commands such as
.B new
and
.B import
otherwise create them on first use.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS mail
.SY "quilt mail"
\fB\-\-mbox\fR \fIfile\fR
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIaddress\fR|\fB\-\-sender\fR \fIaddress\fR
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIprefix\fR]
[\fB\-\-to\fR \fIaddress\fR]
[\fB\-\-cc\fR \fIaddress\fR]
[\fB\-\-bcc\fR \fIaddress\fR]
[\fIfirst_patch\fR [\fIlast_patch\fR]]
.YS
.PP
Write patches from the series, applied or not, to an mbox file with one
message per patch, in the format of
.BR "git format\-patch \%\-\-stdout" ,
for
.BR git\-am (1)
to apply.
Without operands, or with a lone
.BR \- ,
write every patch.
With one patch, write that patch.
With two, write the patches from
.I first_patch
to
.IR last_patch ,
where
.B \-
stands for the first or last patch of the series.
.PP
The first nonblank line of a patch's header becomes the subject, after
.RI [ prefix
.IR n / m ],
or
.RI [ prefix ]
for a lone patch, and the rest of the header becomes the message.
The date is the modification time of the patch file.
Empty patches are skipped with a warning.
.TP
\fB\-\-mbox\fR \fIfile\fR
The file to write, which is replaced if it exists.
As in upstream quilt, a relative name is relative to the root of the tree,
not to the current directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIaddress\fR, \fB\-\-sender\fR \fIaddress\fR
The author, for the
.B From:
header.
One of them is required, and
.B \%\-\-from
wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIprefix\fR
The subject prefix,
.B PATCH
by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR, \fB\-\-cc\fR, \fB\-\-bcc\fR \fIaddress\fR
Add a recipient.
Each may be given more than once.
.PP
For compatibility,
.BR \-m ,
.BR \-M ,
.BR \%\-\-subject ,
.BR \%\-\-reply\-to ,
.BR \%\-\-charset ,
and
.B \%\-\-signature
are accepted, with their values, and ignored.
.B \%\-\-send
is an error.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS new
.SY "quilt new"
[\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR]
\fIpatch\fR
.YS
.PP
Add an empty
.I patch
to the series, after the topmost patch or at the start when none is
applied, and make it the topmost patch.
Its patch file is written by the first refresh.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR
Give the patch strip level 0, or 1, the default.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS next
.SY "quilt next"
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Print the name of the patch after the topmost patch.
Given an unapplied
.IR patch ,
print that patch, and given an applied one, report it and fail.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS patches
.SY "quilt patches"
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]]
\fIfile\fR ...
.YS
.PP
List the patches in the series, applied or not, that change any of the
.IR file s.
For an unapplied patch, its patch file is read.
.TP
.B \-v
Mark the topmost patch with
.B =
and the other applied patches with
.BR + .
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]
Accepted and ignored, as for
.BR diff .
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS pop
.SY "quilt pop"
[\fB\-afRqv\fR]
[\fB\-\-refresh\fR]
[\fInum\fR|\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Remove the topmost patch from the tree, putting back the saved copies of
its files; with
.IR num ,
remove that many patches, and with
.IR patch ,
remove patches until it is the topmost.
.PP
First quilt checks that each patch removes cleanly: the patch file applied to
the saved copies must give the files as they are now.
If not, the patch has changes that were never refreshed, and pop stops.
It also refuses to remove a patch that
.B push \-f
or
.B remove
left needing a refresh.
.TP
.B \-a
Remove all applied patches.
.TP
.B \-f
Remove patches without checking them, losing any changes not refreshed
into them.
.TP
.B \-R
Check each patch, cancelling an earlier
.BR \-f .
quilt.cpp always checks, so this does nothing else.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \%\-\-quiet
Leave out the blank lines and the lines for each file.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \%\-\-verbose
Accepted and ignored, since the files are always listed.
.TP
.B \%\-\-refresh
Refresh each patch before removing it, with the arguments in
.BR \%QUILT_REFRESH_ARGS .
This excludes
.BR \-f .
.PP
The long forms
.B \%\-\-quiet
and
.B \%\-\-verbose
are quilt.cpp extensions.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS previous
.SY "quilt previous"
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Print the name of the patch below the topmost patch, or of the patch before
the named
.I patch
in the series.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS push
.SY "quilt push"
[\fB\-afqv\fR]
[\fB\-m\fR[\fIstyle\fR]|\fB\-\-merge\fR[\fB=\fIstyle\fR]]
[\fB\-\-fuzz=\fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-leave\-rejects\fR]
[\fB\-\-refresh\fR]
[\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]]
[\fInum\fR|\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Apply the next patch in the series; with
.IR num ,
the next
.I num
patches, and with
.IR patch ,
every patch up to and including it.
Before applying a patch, quilt saves a copy of each file it changes.
The strip level, and
.B \-R
for a reversed patch, come from the patch's series entry.
A patch whose file does not exist is applied as an empty patch.
.PP
When a hunk fails to apply, quilt undoes the patch, says whether it would
apply in reverse and so may be applied already, and stops.
Patches applied before it stay applied.
quilt also refuses to apply patches on top of one that
.B \-f
applied, until that patch is refreshed.
.TP
.B \-a
Apply all the remaining patches.
.TP
.B \-f
Apply a failing patch anyway: keep the hunks that apply, write the others to
.IB file .rej
files, and mark the patch as needing a refresh.
Pushing stops there.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \%\-\-quiet
Leave out the blank lines and the lines for each file and hunk.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \%\-\-verbose
When undoing a failed patch, list the files restored and removed.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR[\fIstyle\fR], \fB\-\-merge\fR[\fB=\fIstyle\fR]
Merge a hunk that does not match exactly into the file, between conflict
markers, instead of rejecting it.
.I style
is
.BR merge ,
the default, or
.BR diff3 ,
which also shows the lines the hunk expected.
Fuzz is not used, and a conflict still counts as a failure.
.TP
\fB\-\-fuzz=\fIN\fR
Let a hunk match with up to
.I N
of its first and last context lines ignored, 2 by default.
.TP
.B \%\-\-leave\-rejects
Keep the
.B .rej
files of a patch that failed without
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B \%\-\-refresh
Refresh each patch after applying it, with the arguments in
.BR \%QUILT_REFRESH_ARGS .
This excludes
.BR \-f .
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]
Accepted and ignored, as for
.BR diff .
.PP
The long forms
.B \%\-\-quiet
and
.B \%\-\-verbose
are quilt.cpp extensions.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS refresh
.SY "quilt refresh"
[\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR|\fB\-p ab\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR|\fB\-U\fR \fInum\fR|\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-C\fR \fInum\fR]
[\fB\-z\fR[\fInew_name\fR]]
[\fB\-f\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-timestamps\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-index\fR]
[\fB\-\-diffstat\fR]
[\fB\-\-sort\fR]
[\fB\-\-backup\fR]
[\fB\-\-strip\-trailing\-whitespace\fR]
[\fB\-\-diff\-algorithm=\fIname\fR]
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
Write the patch file of the topmost or named applied patch: its header,
unchanged, then a diff of each file's saved copy against the file as it is
now.
This is how changes get into a patch.
Refreshing also clears the mark that
.B push \-f
and
.B remove
leave, and rewrites the patch's series entry with the strip level used,
dropping any
.BR \-R .
.PP
When a patch applied later also changes one of the files, quilt refuses,
since the file holds that patch's changes too.
Added lines that end in whitespace get a warning.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIn\fR, \fB\-p ab\fR
Label files in the style of
.B \-p0
or
.BR \-p1 ,
or with
.B a/
and
.B b/
prefixes, which apply as
.BR \-p1 ;
see
.BR "Patch files" .
The default is the patch's strip level.
Since
.B ab
is not recorded in the series, give it every time, such as in
.BR \%QUILT_REFRESH_ARGS .
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-U\fR \fInum\fR, \fB\-c\fR, \fB\-C\fR \fInum\fR
Write a unified diff, the default, or a context diff, with 3 or
.I num
lines of context.
.TP
\fB\-z\fR[\fInew_name\fR]
Leave the topmost patch as it is, and put the changes made since its last
refresh into a new patch after it, which becomes the topmost.
Without
.IR new_name ,
the new patch is named as by
.BR fork .
.TP
.B \-f
Refresh even when patches applied later change the same files.
The diff of such a file then stops at the next patch's copy.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-timestamps\fR, \fB\-\-no\-index\fR
Leave out the timestamps, or the
.B Index:
lines.
.TP
.B \%\-\-diffstat
Update the diffstat in the header, or add one at its end.
.TP
.B \%\-\-sort
Sort the files by name, instead of following the old patch file.
.TP
.B \%\-\-backup
Keep the old patch file as
.IB patch \(ti .
.TP
.B \%\-\-strip\-trailing\-whitespace
Remove trailing whitespace from the added lines, in the patch and in the
files themselves.
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\-algorithm=\fIname\fR
As for
.BR diff .
This option is a quilt.cpp extension.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS remove
.SY "quilt remove"
[\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR]
\fIfile\fR ...
.YS
.PP
Take each
.I file
out of the topmost or named applied patch, putting back its saved copy, or
deleting it if the patch created it.
Since the patch file still holds the file's changes, the patch then needs a
refresh before it can be popped.
quilt reports a file that is not in the patch, or that a patch applied later
also changes, and goes on with the rest.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS rename
.SY "quilt rename"
[\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR]
\fInew_name\fR
.YS
.PP
Rename the topmost or named patch, applied or not, and its patch file.
The new name must not be in use.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS revert
.SY "quilt revert"
[\fB\-P\fR \fIpatch\fR]
\fIfile\fR ...
.YS
.PP
Undo the changes to each
.I file
since the topmost or named applied patch was last refreshed, making the file
as the patch file has it.
Nothing is reverted if any file is not in the patch, or is also changed by a
patch applied later.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS series
.SY "quilt series"
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]]
.YS
.PP
List all patches in the series.
.TP
.B \-v
Mark the topmost patch with
.B =
and the other applied patches with
.BR + .
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[\fB=\fIwhen\fR]
Accepted and ignored, as for
.BR diff .
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS snapshot
.SY "quilt snapshot"
[\fB\-d\fR]
.YS
.PP
Save a copy of each file in the applied patches, replacing any earlier
snapshot, so that
.B quilt diff \%\-\-snapshot
can later show what has changed since.
.TP
.B \-d
Remove the snapshot instead.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS top
.SY "quilt top"
.YS
.PP
Print the name of the topmost patch.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS unapplied
.SY "quilt unapplied"
[\fIpatch\fR]
.YS
.PP
List the patches that follow the topmost patch, or the named
.IR patch ,
in the series.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS upgrade
.SY "quilt upgrade"
.YS
.PP
Do nothing.
Upstream quilt converts older
.I .pc
formats, and quilt.cpp knows only the current one.
.\" ------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error, such as a usage error or a patch that does not apply.
.TP
.B 2
Nothing to do.
.B push
exits 2 when no patch is left to apply or the named patch is already
applied, and
.B pop
when it has nothing to remove.
.B top
exits 2 when no patch is applied,
.B next
when no patch follows or the named patch is applied, and
.B previous
when the topmost or named patch is the first.
.B add
exits 2 when a file is already in the patch, and nothing else failed.
.PP
So
.B while quilt push; do quilt refresh; done
stops at the end of the series.
.B edit
exits with the editor's status.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Variables set in the configuration file override the environment.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_PATCHES
The patches directory, relative to the root or absolute;
.B patches
by default.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_SERIES
The name of the series file;
.B series
by default.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_PC
The directory for quilt's state, relative to the root;
.B .pc
by default.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_PATCHES_PREFIX
If not empty, print patch names with the patches directory in front,
relative to the current directory, such as
.BR patches/fix.patch .
.TP
.BI \%QUILT_ COMMAND _ARGS
Default arguments for
.IR command ,
named in capitals, such as
.B \%QUILT_PUSH_ARGS
for
.BR push .
They come before the arguments on the command line, and are split into
words as by a shell, with quotes,
.BI $ name
and
.BI ${ name }\fR,
and backslash escapes, but nothing more.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_DIFF_OPTS
Options for diffs.
Of those of GNU
.BR diff (1),
quilt.cpp understands
.BI \-U " num"\fR,
which overrides the context given on the command line, and
.BR \%\-\-strip\-trailing\-cr ,
and ignores the rest.
With
.BR \%\-\-strip\-trailing\-cr ,
a carriage return before a line feed is ignored, so patches to files with
CRLF line endings are written with LF endings, and quilt.cpp applies them
to such files keeping the CRLF endings.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_PATCH_OPTS
Options for applying patches.
Of those of GNU
.BR patch (1),
quilt.cpp understands
.B \-R
to reverse,
.B \-s
to be quiet,
.B \-E
to delete files left empty, and
.BI \%\-\-fuzz= N\fR,
and ignores the rest.
.TP
.B \%QUILT_DIFF_ALGORITHM
The diff algorithm, unless
.B \%\-\-diff\-algorithm
gives one:
.B myers
by default, which limits its search to stay fast on large, much changed
files;
.BR minimal ,
the same without the limit, for the shortest diff;
.BR patience ,
which lines files up on the lines that occur once in each; or
.BR histogram ,
which also uses lines that are merely rare.
This variable is a quilt.cpp extension, which upstream quilt ignores.
.TP
.BR \%QUILT_NO_DIFF_INDEX ", " \%QUILT_NO_DIFF_TIMESTAMPS
If not empty, act as
.B \%\-\-no\-index
or
.B \%\-\-no\-timestamps
does for every diff and refresh.
.TP
.B EDITOR
The editor for
.B edit
and
.BR "header \-e" ,
split into words at blanks, so that it may hold options, and run with the
files after them;
.B vi
by default.
.TP
.B HOME
Where to find
.IR .quiltrc .
On Windows, when it is unset,
.B \%USERPROFILE
is used.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where
.B diff \-z
and
.B header \-e
put temporary files;
.I /tmp
by default.
.PP
quilt.cpp ignores the upstream variables
.BR \%QUILT_COLORS ,
.BR \%QUILT_PAGER ,
.BR LESS ,
and
.BR \%QUILT_DIFFSTAT_OPTS .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I patches/
The patch files, in the directory named by
.BR \%QUILT_PATCHES .
.TP
.I patches/series
The series file.
quilt looks for it as
.IR .pc/series ,
as
.I series
in the root, and as
.IR patches/series ,
and creates it in the patches directory.
.TP
.I .pc/
quilt's state, in the directory named by
.BR \%QUILT_PC :
.RS
.TP
.I applied\-patches
The applied patches, from the bottom of the stack up.
.TP
.IR .version ", " .quilt_patches ", " .quilt_series
The format version, 2, and the patches directory and series file name in
use when
.I .pc
was created, which then take precedence over the environment.
.TP
.IR patch /
The copies of files saved for
.IR patch ,
where an empty copy stands for a file that did not exist, and
.IR .timestamp .
.TP
.IB patch \(tirefresh
The mark of a patch that needs a refresh before it pops cleanly, left by
.B push \-f
or
.BR remove .
.TP
.I .snap/
The snapshot.
.RE
.TP
.IB file .rej
The hunks of a patch that did not apply.
.TP
.IB patch \(ti
An old patch file kept by
.BR \%\-\-backup .
.TP
.IR \(ti/.quiltrc ", " /etc/quilt.quiltrc
The configuration file: the first of these that is not empty, unless
.B \%\-\-quiltrc
gives another.
On Windows, the second is
.I ..\(rsetc\(rsquilt.quiltrc
relative to the directory of
.BR quilt.exe .
.SS Series file
Each line names a patch, relative to the patches directory, and may follow
it with options:
.BI \-p num
or
.BI "\-p " num
for the strip level, 1 by default, and
.B \-R
for a patch that applies in reverse.
Blank lines are ignored, and so are comments: a line that starts with
.BR # ,
or the rest of a line from a space followed by
.BR # .
Commands change only the line of the patch concerned, keeping comments and
formatting.
The file may be edited by hand, as long as the applied patches stay first,
in their order.
Otherwise every command but
.B pop
refuses to run until the patches are popped.
.SS Patch files
A patch file begins with a header of free text, such as a description,
which quilt keeps as it is.
The diff starts at the first line that begins with
.B Index:
or
.BR "diff \-" ,
or at a pair of file name lines:
.B \-\-\-
then
.BR +++ ,
or for a context diff,
.B ***
then
.BR \-\-\- .
Each file's diff begins with an
.B Index:
line and a line of
.BR = ,
and its file name lines carry modification times, unless
.B \%\-\-no\-index
and
.B \%\-\-no\-timestamps
leave them out.
.PP
For a file
.I f
in a tree whose root directory is named
.IR d ,
the file name lines read:
.TP
.B \-p0
.IB f .orig
and
.IR f .
.TP
.B \-p1
.IB d .orig/ f
and
.IB d / f\fR,
the default.
.TP
.B \-p ab
.BI a/ f
and
.BI b/ f\fR,
as
.BR git\-diff (1)
writes them.
.PP
A created or deleted file is
.B /dev/null
on its missing side.
A binary file cannot be diffed: a changed file with a NUL byte in its first
8192 bytes makes diff and refresh fail.
.SS Configuration file
The configuration file sets the variables in
.BR ENVIRONMENT ,
one per line, as
.IB name = value\fR,
optionally after
.BR export .
A value may be in single quotes, taken literally, or in double quotes,
where
.B \e"
and
.B \e\e
stand for
.B """"
and
.BR \e ;
an unquoted value ends at a blank or a
.BR # .
quilt.cpp does not run the file as upstream quilt does, with
.BR bash (1).
It ignores comments and every line that is not an assignment, but applies
each assignment, even one inside an
.B if
or a loop.
The variables are put in the environment, overriding values already there.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a new patch that changes a file, and describe it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quilt new fix\-overflow.patch
quilt edit src/parse.c
quilt refresh
quilt header \-e
.EE
.RE
.PP
Refresh every patch after the code under them has changed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quilt pop \-a
while quilt push; do quilt refresh; done
.EE
.RE
.PP
Add a patch from elsewhere, and apply it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quilt import /tmp/upstream\-fix.patch
quilt push
.EE
.RE
.PP
Apply the whole series to a Git repository as commits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quilt mail \-\-mbox /tmp/series.mbox \e
    \-\-from \(aqA. U. Thor <author@example.com>\(aq
git \-C ../repo am /tmp/series.mbox
.EE
.RE
.PP
Draw the dependencies of all applied patches:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
quilt graph \-\-all \-\-reduce | dot \-Tsvg >deps.svg
.EE
.RE
.PP
Settings for Debian packages, in
.IR \(ti/.quiltrc :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
QUILT_PATCHES=debian/patches
QUILT_REFRESH_ARGS="\-p ab \-\-no\-timestamps \-\-no\-index"
QUILT_DIFF_ARGS="\-p ab \-\-no\-timestamps \-\-no\-index"
.EE
.RE
.SH COMPATIBILITY
quilt.cpp follows quilt 0.69, with these differences.
.IP \(bu 2
The diff and patch engines are built in, and
.B \%QUILT_DIFF_OPTS
and
.B \%QUILT_PATCH_OPTS
take only the options listed in
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.B refresh \%\-\-diffstat
needs no
.BR diffstat (1).
.IP \(bu
Output is never colored or paged.
.IP \(bu
.BR grep ,
.BR setup ,
.BR shell ,
and
.B graph \-T ps
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu
.B mail
writes an mbox for
.BR git\-am (1),
instead of mailing patches with a cover letter: it needs
.B \%\-\-mbox
and an author, refuses
.BR \%\-\-send ,
and ignores the options for the cover letter.
.IP \(bu
The configuration file is read, not run, and quilt.cpp splits
.BI \%QUILT_ COMMAND _ARGS
with its own subset of shell syntax.
.IP \(bu
.B init
and
.B header \%\-\-dep3
come from Debian's quilt.
.BR \%\-\-diff\-algorithm ,
.BR \%QUILT_DIFF_ALGORITHM ,
.B \%\-\-quiet
and
.B \%\-\-verbose
for push and pop, and
.B \-h
and
.B \%\-\-help
for every command are quilt.cpp's own.
.IP \(bu
.B fork
and
.B refresh \-z
count a
.BI \- N
suffix up in decimal, where quilt reads one with a leading zero as octal.
A name given to
.B refresh \-z
may begin with the patches directory, as for
.B new
and
.BR fork .
.IP \(bu
When
.B import \-f
replaces a patch that has the same header, the header is not repeated, and
the choice of header is made for each patch on its own.
.IP \(bu
.B upgrade
does nothing, and
.B \%\-\-trace
is ignored.
.SH HISTORY
quilt started as a set of scripts by Andrew Morton, which Andreas
\%Gruenbacher rewrote.
quilt.cpp is an independent reimplementation of it in C++, as a single
program that also runs natively on Windows.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1),
.BR patch (1),
.BR git\-am (1),
.BR git\-format\-patch (1),
.BR dot (1)
.PP
Upstream quilt:
.I https://savannah.nongnu.org/projects/quilt
